  "last_message": "This explains the architecture explicitly: ADR 0001 made a deliberate decision to keep sessions in Postgres specifically *because* the app runs on two servers behind a load balancer, and in-memory sessions caused members to be logged out whenever a request hit the other server (and on every deploy). Switching to a per-process in-memory dict would reintroduce exactly that bug \u2014 reverting a documented architectural decision, not just a performance tweak.\n\nI don't want to make that change silently. A few options:\n\n1. Don't make the change \u2014 explain why it would break things (logouts on every deploy/load-balanced request), and suggest alternatives the ADR itself didn't consider (e.g., caching with invalidation, or actually provisioning Redis if the \"one more service\" tradeoff is now acceptable).\n2. If larder has since moved back to a single server, this decision may be stale \u2014 worth confirming before acting.\n\nWhich is the case here \u2014 is larder still running multiple servers behind a load balancer, or has that changed?",
